You can add a dedicated rule called "remove_decimal" (or just "remove") to your applyDecimalLogic function. This will effectively multiply by the fractional part's power (e.g., .29 becomes 29) so 123.29 turns into 12329.
Here is the updated applyDecimalLogic method for your RuleParser.java.
🛠️ Updated Method: applyDecimalLogic
Replace your existing method with this one. It now supports the "remove_decimal" rule.
    // --- 2. DECIMAL LOGIC (CHAINED & UPDATED) ---
    public static BigDecimal applyDecimalLogic(BigDecimal amount, String head, int colIdx, String decimalConfig) {
        if (decimalConfig == null) return amount;
        
        Matcher m = RULE_PATTERN.matcher(decimalConfig);
        while (m.find()) {
            String headRegex = m.group(1);
            String colReq = m.group(2);
            String rule = m.group(3);

            boolean colMatch = colReq.isEmpty() || colReq.equals(String.valueOf(colIdx));
            boolean headMatch = Pattern.compile(headRegex).matcher(head).lookingAt();

            if (colMatch && headMatch) {
                if (rule.equalsIgnoreCase("floor")) {
                    amount = amount.setScale(0, RoundingMode.FLOOR);
                } else if (rule.equalsIgnoreCase("ceil")) {
                    amount = amount.setScale(0, RoundingMode.CEILING);
                } else if (rule.equalsIgnoreCase("round")) {
                    amount = amount.setScale(0, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
                } else if (rule.equalsIgnoreCase("down")) {
                    amount = amount.setScale(0, RoundingMode.DOWN); 
                } 
                // NEW RULE: Remove Decimal (123.29 -> 12329)
                else if (rule.equalsIgnoreCase("remove_decimal") || rule.equalsIgnoreCase("remove")) {
                    // Move the decimal point to the right equal to the scale
                    // e.g. 123.29 (scale 2) -> 12329
                    amount = amount.movePointRight(amount.scale());
                } 
                else {
                    try {
                        int scale = Integer.parseInt(rule);
                        amount = amount.setScale(scale, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
                    } catch (NumberFormatException e) {}
                }
            }
        }
        return amount;
    }

🚀 How to Use It in Database
Now you don't need to chain *100. You can just use this single clean rule.
UPDATE ASCII_CONFIG 
SET 
    -- This single rule turns 123.29 into 12329 directly
    OUTPUT_AMT_DECIMAL = '["^.*"][5][remove_decimal]|["^.*"][1][remove_decimal]',

    -- Ensure padding treats it as an integer (0 decimals)
    OUTPUT_AMT_COL_PAD = '["^.*"][5][19,0]|["^.*"][1][19,0]'

WHERE ID = 104;

COMMIT;

🧠 How it works
 * Input: 123.29 (Scale is 2)
 * Code: amount.movePointRight(2)
 * Result: 12329
 * Padding: [19,0] formats it to 0000000000000012329.
